ARM7TDMI 66MHz timing libraries
===============================

Generic timing libraries for a 66MHz ARM7TDMI are supplied for use
during synthesis, but must first be compiled from .lib format into
synopsys format .db files.

This is done using the commands 'read_lib' and 'write_lib' in
Synopsys Design Compiler.

Supplying the libraries in this format allows changes to be made to
the core timings which is not possible with compiled .db libraries.

Pre-compiled .db versions of the libraries are also supplied.


Modifications to the default LIB file
=====================================

There are some timing arcs in the standard ARM7TDMI LIB files which
can cause problems in synthesis.  This is due to the setup and hold
arcs for an input pin being referenced to opposite edges of the clock,
and occurs on ALE, APE and BL[3:0].  Without modification, these arcs
can cause Synopsys Design-Compiler to try to fix a hold violation on a
path which starts on one edge of the clock yet finishes on the other - 
i.e. Design-Compiler believes that there is a hold violation of an
entire phase of the clock.

The supplied LIB files have been edited as follows:

MAX LIB file: comment out the hold timing arcs for ALE, APE, BL[3:0]

MIN LIB file: comment out the setup timing arcs for ALE, APE, BL[3:0]

Once modified in this way, the two LIB files no longer contain the
same timing arcs.  Loading them both into Design-Compiler or Primetime 
may cause warnings: they should only be loaded one at a time.

WARNING: When writing out final SDF (either from Design-Compiler, or
         from a layout tool such as Avant! Apollo which can accept
         Synopsys LIB files) the relevant unmodified LIB file should
         be used.  This is to ensure that all paths which may be
         referenced later in the design flow have associated timing in 
         the SDF file.
